the city of New Orleans and the culinary  world are remembering legendary chef  Leah chase her career spanned more than  seven decades she created a safe haven  for civil rights activists including dr.  Martin Luther King jr. CBS this morning  Saturday co-host Michelle Miller first  met mrs. chase more than 25 years ago  Michelle she also became a good friend I  know she absolutely did when I first met  mrs. chase she was already a giant in  the culinary world her signature dishes  of Louisiana gumbo southern fried  chicken and shrimp Clemons Oh brought  her worldwide fame but there was so much  more than food that made her an American  legend Leah chase was called the queen  of Creole cuisine and her palace was  Dooky Chase's if I wouldn't come in this  kitchen every day I think I would be  miserable born in 1923 chase started out  as a waitress in the French Quarter  before marrying Edgar ducky chase jr. a  musician whose parents ran a small  sandwich shop mrs. chase took over and  transformed it into a fine-dining  restaurant for African Americans in the  1950s what did this place mean this was  like a safe haven for all of us during  the civil rights era chase broke  segregation laws by providing a space  for both black and white activists to  meet in 2005 Hurricane Katrina destroyed  Dooky chase but chase reopened it two  years later people said well what are  you gonna do at your age there was only  one thing for me to do no matter what  you do on this earth you have to do it  do it well  Ray Charles thing about her  and she even served as the inspiration  behind Disney's first african-american  princess I cannot believe I'm doing this  her customers included Martin Luther  King jr. and two US presidents George W  Bush and Barack Obama whom Chase had to  chastise I gave him the gumbo first  thing he does take the hot sauce  you don't put hot sauce in my gumbo in  2016  Chase received a Lifetime Achievement  Award from the James Beard Foundation oh  my goodness